Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204083 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204083 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204084 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1045 "Newton's Method for Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186205019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204161 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "96759bff-642a-50d7-9380-eaafb12358e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1069 "q-255856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-3}} | $3$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $0.833$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76b77696-fb71-5bf3-bbfc-fa9d52e3daf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204494 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4(1) & 2(1) \\\\ 3(1)^{2} & 3(1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-2 \\\\ (1)^{3}+(1)^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.833 \\\\ 0.833\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186204499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186202982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204499 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+x-3 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}\\,≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1.091$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2d3b902-846f-5938-a220-235340dd6162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204499 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+x-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+x-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+x-3 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+x-3 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(2)+1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2)-1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}(2)+(1)-3 \\\\ (1)(2)^{2}-(2)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{11} \\\\ \\frac{17}{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.091 \\\\ 1.545\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186203013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186203014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204509 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ {x}^{3}y-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-1}}\n        </td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-2}}\n        </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5800980a-2f76-57ac-b91f-63be30605046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204509 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & -4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ {x}^{3}y-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ {x}^{3}y-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1) & -4(1)^{3} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}(1) & (1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-(1)^{4}+1 \\\\ (1)^{3}(1)-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-(-4)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205019 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205019 #edb/ref 1061 460080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205019 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f8755c4-151c-5717-bad9-4b19facb02ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205019 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205020 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205020 #edb/ref 1061 460081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205020 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c949ec22-4c82-5a5a-bc81-fad6683bf0b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205020 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205021 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205021 #edb/ref 1061 460082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205021 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e285dc9f-9805-5b95-bd83-c6f8da901a74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205021 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186204082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205022 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205022 #edb/ref 1061 460085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205022 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9429df1a-24e6-5bf3-ba31-93d9bf4d2b34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205022 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186204083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205023 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205023 #edb/ref 1061 460088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205023 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16956ca3-9f37-53de-bdb3-838d68b2c7f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205023 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205024 #edb/ref 1061 460089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205024 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aac6d91b-f746-5467-8c5a-9b33cfe7ff06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205024 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205025 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205025 #edb/ref 1061 460090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205025 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cc729fd-23d2-528a-b73a-daf856e2f710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205025 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186204084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205026 #edb/ref 1061 460093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205026 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5c82352-d99e-5c17-9e33-330fd65ee50e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205026 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205143 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how Newton’s method works for functions of a single variable. Given a function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, the method generates a sequence of approximations using the rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.\n$$\n\nThis formula uses the derivative $f'(x_n)$ to construct a local linear approximation of $f$ near $x_n$, which we then use to refine our guess.\n\nIn this lesson, we turn to the multivariable case, where we want to solve systems of nonlinear equations. Specifically, we are interested in finding a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ that solves\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nfor a vector-valued function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nThe multivariable version of Newton’s method updates our guess using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian matrix of $\\boldsymbol{f}$, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nThis is a natural extension of the single-variable rule, replacing the derivative with the Jacobian and the scalar update with a vector.\n\nTo see how this method works in practice, let’s walk through a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205143 #edb/ref 1065 10772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205143 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fc814a9d-5637-521d-a01f-90d4a23e215c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205143 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205157 #edb/ref 1064 "Now that we’ve seen how to apply Newton’s method in the multivariable case, it’s worth noting that the standard update formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nis not always the most practical way for us to compute the next step. In higher dimensions, it’s often more useful to avoid calculating the inverse of a large matrix by rewriting the update as a linear system instead.\n\nLet’s go through how we can do that step by step.\n\nWe start with the standard Newton update\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $\\mathbf{x}_n$ from both sides\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n = - [J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides by $J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$\n\n$$\nJ_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nThis gives us the *linear system form* of Newton’s method. This equivalent form allows us to solve directly for the update direction $\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n$ without ever computing the inverse of the Jacobian.\n\nTo better understand how the method works in practice, let’s walk through a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205157 #edb/ref 1065 10905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205157 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ad00df1c-99b0-5fa7-96ee-f43a94557f73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205157 #edb/ref 1067 "Alternative to Newton's Method for Multivariable Functions Using Linear Systems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205201 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are looking for a zero of the function\n![](images/18/18f60cbe76057bd9812b4d582c15e52947eb0aa093b99b9968f37a3dd7e6935b.svg)\nin the region $[-2, 2] \\times [-2, 2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nWe will perform one iteration of the Newton–Raphson method, starting from the initial guess\n![](images/da/da4b9825035876734349256d75dd639a6a01224cecd69657a63a7521c7834394.svg)\n\nThe Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+5) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2x & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/d3/d3c2bba099548c681d1b62a3c4743c488ea2cc016e9f2dda97cdfb2592c997d8.svg)\n\nStarting with ![](images/1d/1da6531e25c9f79687ab4d05ecc7b3817a90ba5ce27c73f092cbb09a8810043b.svg) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2x & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{3}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{3} & -2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & -3(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{4}-(1)^{2}+5 \\\\ (1)^{2}-(1)^{3}+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.625 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205201 #edb/ref 1065 13125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205201 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9a1704a-b2f2-58ca-8eb8-cfe28a10b296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205201 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205202 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are looking for a zero of the function\n![](images/b5/b5cd4e117432b529b2c149a3f5f83b40ab3909e9b95cb8929942bbca32a023d1.svg)\nin the region $[-2, 2] \\times [-2, 3] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nThe Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\nJ_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}-\\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+5) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2x & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](images/a5/a51051e38e93233589296054cc501eb7c6093cf1bd21530f0568defdb04a981f.svg)\n\nStarting with ![](images/e3/e33a9bd1d7579b939006809f41ab761b348d0517cd4cdf9205b62e67b64ff98e.svg) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2x & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{3}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{3} & -2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & -3(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{4}-(1)^{2}+5 \\\\ (1)^{2}-(1)^{3}+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet’s solve this system using Gaussian elimination. We first set ![](images/97/9793fe5320b84146f78a87361e933a35a7c8386c80a978c2d8c75c2b496d19aa.svg), so the system becomes\n![](images/31/31e3b8ee7dd61752140fab134e85fdb4cbb512d6abb0ca69d6e6ee166513e2ff.svg)\n\nWe write the augmented matrix as\n![](images/b9/b99dbe7304b2937f64f5072ae8c46295b0a49884fdc191d1d60935a0d32a4c43.svg)\n\nTo eliminate the entry below the leading $4$, we perform the row operation $R_2 := R_2 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_1{:}$\n![](images/41/41e00b979fcaf46c4650aca61d2a5acdbeb05c64688552c0e040f92d6b30a194.svg)\n\nFrom the second row, we get\n\n$$\n-2d_2 = 1.5 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad d_2 = -0.75.\n$$\n\nWe then substitute this into the first row, which gives\n\n$$\n4d_1 - 2(-0.75) = -5 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 4d_1 = -6.5 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad d_1 = -1.625.\n$$\n\nSo, the update vector is\n![](images/d2/d20f59f6293e504d26e6730407ab33a5d35ccad9fdbc9b01df0dc706c20d6b50.svg)\n\nHence, our updated approximation of the root is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-1.625 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1.625 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.625 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205202 #edb/ref 1065 13126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205202 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7de67ce-53d0-56ee-aa95-84dde9560679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205202 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205205 #edb/ref 1064 "Just like in the single-variable case, the idea is to simplify the problem by replacing a nonlinear function with its linear approximation near our current guess.\n\nSuppose we want to solve a system of nonlinear equations\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a vector-valued function.\n\nNear a point $\\mathbf{x}_0$, we approximate $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ using its first-order Taylor expansion:\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_0) + J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_0)(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_0),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_0)$ is the Jacobian matrix of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_0$.\n\nSince this linear approximation is valid near $\\mathbf{x}_0$, we solve the simpler system\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_0) + J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_0)(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_0) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ninstead of the full nonlinear system.\n\nTo find the next guess $\\mathbf{x}_1$, we solve this linear system for $\\mathbf{x}$. We isolate the term $\\mathbf{x}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}({x}_{0})(x-{x}_{0}) & =-f({x}_{0}) \\\\ x-{x}_{0} & =-[{J}_{f}({x}_{0})]^{-1}f({x}_{0}) \\\\ x & ={x}_{0}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{0})]^{-1}f({x}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new value becomes our next guess, which we call $\\mathbf{x}_1$. So, we write\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_{\\boldsymbol{f}}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nThis is the multivariable version of the Newton–Raphson update rule." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205205 #edb/ref 1065 13227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205205 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "729dee84-09dc-5611-8787-27a3cc455d8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205205 #edb/ref 1067 "Derivation of the Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290871 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290871 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290871 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39c393a2-8fbe-5